 ## Background
-Dynamic and temporal modeling in volume rendering originated from the need to extend static NeRF and 3DGS representations to 4D scenes. D-NeRF [S1, S3] was one of the early methods to introduce deformation networks for non-rigid scenes. Subsequent works like DaReNeRF [S1] and OD-NeRF [S1] improved efficiency and handling of dynamic artifacts. In the context of 3D Gaussian Splatting, methods like Temporal 3DGS [S1], SWAGS [S1], and 3DGStream [S1] adapted the explicit Gaussian representation to handle dynamics through temporal basis functions, windowed training, and neural caches. The field has also seen applications in medical imaging, such as infant motion reconstruction [S1].
+Dynamic scene reconstruction has evolved from explicit deformation fields to implicit neural representations and recently to dynamic 3D Gaussians. D-NeRF [S1, S3] was a foundational work that introduced the canonical space deformation approach for NeRFs, allowing novel view synthesis of dynamic scenes from monocular inputs. Subsequent works like DaReNeRF [S1] addressed limitations in wavelet-based decompositions for dynamic volumes. The advent of 3D Gaussian Splatting [S1] spurred dynamic variants such as Temporal 3DGS, SWAGS, and 3DGStream, which adapt the explicit Gaussian representation to handle motion through temporal attributes, windowed canonical spaces, or neural caches [S1]. More recent approaches like OD-NeRF [S1] explore on-the-fly training with color-guided conditioning to reduce computational overhead. These methods are applied in medical imaging, such as infant motion reconstruction [S1], and general dynamic scene streaming [S1].
 
